<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>P(t)</m> describes the population
        of a particular species, where <m>P</m> is
        represented in millions.  Suppose also that the
        change in the population is determined by the
        differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dP}{dt}=f(P)
        </md>
        where <m>f(P)</m> is the function graphed below.
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
        <shordescription>A graph of the function f of P</shordescription>
        <xi:include href="prefigure/27-graph.xml"/>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For what values of the population <m>P</m>
          does the population increase?  For what values
          of the population <m>P</m> does the population
          decrease?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          From the graph, <m>f(P) \gt 0</m> for
          <m>1 \lt P \lt 3</m>, so the population
          increases in this range.  <m>f(P) \lt 0</m>
          for <m>0 \lt P \lt 1</m> or <m>P \gt 3</m>,
          so the population decreases in these ranges.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify any equilibrium populations.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          The equilibrium populations are <m>P=0</m>,
          <m>P=1</m>, and <m>P=3</m> million, which are the places
          where <m>f(P)=0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch a slope field for the differential
          equation <m>\frac{dP}{dt} = f(P)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <image width="40%">
          <shortdescription>The slope field of the differential equation</shortdescription>
          <xi:include href="prefigure/27-slope.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the equilibrium populations
          you have found are either stable or unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          <m>P=0</m> and <m>P=3</m> are stable
          equilibria and <m>P=1</m> is unstable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>P(0) \lt 1</m>, what happens to this
          species eventually?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          If <m>P(0) \lt 1</m>, the population is below
          the threshold and decreases to extinction
          (<m>P \to 0</m>).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>P(0) \gt 1</m>, what happens eventually?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          If <m>P(0) \gt 1</m>, the population is above
          the threshold and eventually stabilizes at the
          carrying capacity <m>P=3</m> million.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>P(t)</m> describes the population
        of a particular species of fish in a lake where
        <m>P</m> is measured in thousands and <m>t</m>
        is measured in months.  Suppose that the change
        in the population is given by the differential
        equation
        <md>
          \frac{dP}{dt} = P(6-P).
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph of the function
          <m>f(P) = P(6-P)</m> and use it to determine
          any equilibrium solutions.  Determine whether
          these equilibria are stable or unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <image width="40%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/27-de-2.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          The equilibria are <m>P=0</m>
          (unstable, since nearby positive populations
          move away) and <m>P=6</m> (stable).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to the population of fish
          eventually?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          If <m>P(0) \gt 0</m>, the fish population
          eventually stabilizes at <m>P=6</m> thousand.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that the lake is opened to fishing
          and that one thousand fish per month are
          removed from the lake (remember that <m>P</m>
          is measured in <em>thousands</em> of fish).
          Modify the differential equation to take this
          into account.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          <md>
            \frac{dP}{dt} = P(6-P) - 1.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the new equilibrium solutions and
          their stability.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          Setting <m>P(6-P) - 1 = 0</m> gives
          <m>P^2 - 6P + 1 = 0</m>, so
          <m>P = 3 \pm 2\sqrt{2}</m>.  The upper
          equilibrium <m>3+2\sqrt{2} \approx 5.83</m>
          is stable; the lower equilibrium
          <m>3-2\sqrt{2} \approx 0.17</m> is unstable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to the fish after a long time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="27">
        <p>
          If the initial population exceeds
          <m>0.17</m> thousand,
          the population stabilizes around
          <m>5.83</m> thousand
          fish.  If the initial population begins below <m>0.17</m>
          thousand, then the fish go extinct.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
